#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199334} :activity/id #uuid "e864b1f7-a24d-5b18-b4a7-c727177eb578" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049333 :topic/id #uuid "ec9aa34d-69be-5e48-a2c9-562d23408d0d" :topic/math-academy-id 4167} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198050 :question/id #uuid "cb13bd5e-ec8a-5e5c-a45f-d710ced8c3ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-12473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b2c4733-27b9-52be-b326-b4360580b685" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050326}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281593}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of a Rod About the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "482d14bd-b9c9-5f57-b73c-1c1f49d74d92" :step/math-academy-id 44913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198051 :question/id #uuid "4ebe8c87-95ba-58e2-b5d4-1bb19f231dc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12474"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56762142-e975-5cd2-8699-3bf7a9605e93" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281570}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Center of Mass of a Rod"} :step/id #uuid "af9ac3a2-f3c4-5021-867e-f5efa7e918da" :step/math-academy-id 44914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198410 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198052 :question/id #uuid "5c469b72-7ee3-5084-be63-2b7e62b42146" :question/math-academy-id "e-12475"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70278fe3-d8dd-5f72-8bf7-f1a4a17be526" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281573}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of a Thin Rod"} :step/id #uuid "7574e535-619b-5fb0-b185-be9a15178e66" :step/math-academy-id 44915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199486 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin rod whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution along a thin rod using a so-called **mass density function** $\\lambda (x).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit length* at any point $x$ on the rod. The units of $\\lambda$ are usually $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}.$\n\nConsider a thin rod between the points $x=1$ and $x=3$ along the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ee70d28476ebda61ccce5c6342a7f1a48a3e0f4dc26ee1af147d6f3d34a218.png)\n\nSuppose the mass density function of the rod is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x) = 3x^2 \\, \\textrm{kg/m},\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $x \\in (1,3)$ on the rod and cut out a small piece of length $\\Delta{x},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b62c29b499159fe23e9ece7023ed179f4565600a88677fe51ab002a4a0d6faf.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the mass of this piece is\n\n$$\n\\Delta{m} \\approx 3x^2 \\Delta{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\Delta{m}$ are \n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\textrm{m} = \\textrm{kg},$\nas we'd expect.\n\nTo find the total mass of the rod, we add together the masses of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nm =  \\int_{1}^{3} 3x^2 \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the total mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =26\\,\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b,$ if $\\lambda(x)$ denotes the rod's mass density function, then the total mass of the rod is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\int_a^b \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d97cfb9-13c8-5638-8080-e1c8cd1b90f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7760 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57e92bfb-7b99-5c98-ae8c-4999964eb518" :step/math-academy-id 44917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199490 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how to calculate the total moment about the origin of a collection of masses distributed along the $x$-axis. We'll now extend our understanding to include moments of thin rods.\n\nConsider a thin rod between the points $x=1$ and $x=3$ along the $x$-axis with mass density function $\\lambda(x) = 3x^2 \\, \\textrm{kg/m},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ee70d28476ebda61ccce5c6342a7f1a48a3e0f4dc26ee1af147d6f3d34a218.png)\n\nHow can we find the total moment of the rod about the origin?\n\nTo answer this, we once again consider a small piece of rod located in the interval $(x, x+\\Delta x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd2dc58c503333a365b140de3ef1b559477788953dab14549478ca031df98125.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can approximate this piece as a point mass. \n\nThe moment of this piece about the origin, denoted $\\Delta{M_O},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{O} & \\approx x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & \\approx x\\cdot \\lambda (x)Δx \\\\  & =x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, to find the moment of the entire rod about the origin, we sum the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta x.$ By doing so, we get \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_{1}^{3} 3x^3 \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we find that the total moment about $O$ of the rod is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(1)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =60\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn general, the moment about the origin of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "23310936-fde8-5269-917c-4aec2c5591cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7776 :tutorial/title "The Moment of a Rod About the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "459d7e8d-a9ee-5771-a2c6-38ae023189cb" :step/math-academy-id 45028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199491 :tutorial/content "A thin rod's **center of mass** is the point $\\bar{x}$ on the $x$-axis where the system's total mass could be concentrated to give the same moment as the original rod. \n\nAnalogous to the case of point masses, the center of mass of a thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the mass of the rod, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "05cbd84c-3413-5883-bf92-6da730395e8e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7777 :tutorial/title "The Center of Mass of a Rod"} :step/id #uuid "a8a2d02d-5f5b-5ea7-8825-3d994a093207" :step/math-academy-id 45029 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199332}}] :activity/title "Moments and Centers of Mass of Thin Rods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198496}]}
